## What a SPA gives up

Scaffolded with `--rendering spa`, this app has no server layer. The router still does
its job in the browser — the auth guard still bounces `/account` to `/login`,
`/home` still redirects to `/`, and an unmatched path still renders the
not-found page. What is missing is everything only a server can do:

- **No HTTP status on a cold request.** Those three outcomes are 200s to a
  crawler or a `curl`; only in-app navigation sees them as redirects.
- **No `/sitemap.xml`.**
- **No server-painted first paint.** `<html lang dir>` and the theme class are
  applied by React on mount, so the first frame uses the defaults.
- **Static hosting needs a history-API fallback** — every unmatched path must
  serve `index.html`, or deep links 404 at the CDN.

Re-scaffold without `--rendering spa` to get the SSR layer back.

<% } -%>
## Adding routes

`summon` scaffolds the building blocks of the app so new features follow the
established structure rather than diverging from it.

```bash
summon domain billing            # a new feature domain (routes + pages)
summon route billing/invoices    # a route within a domain
summon wrapper sidebar           # a layout wrapper
```

## Testing

```bash
bun run test          # unit + component tests (Vitest, jsdom)
bun run test:coverage # the same, with a coverage report
<% if (spa) { -%>
